\section{Theory, pass 1}
A big difference between coxph and cmsh is how the likelihood is constructed.
The semiparametric MSH (coxph) is a model for observed \emph{transitions}, 
while the interval censored (cmsh) approach is a model for observed
\emph{states}.
The semiparametric model assumes that we see all of the transitions, up to
the point they are censored.  It uses those transition and their timings,
via risk sets, to construct estimated rates. 
These rates can the be used to compte probability in state curves $p(t)$.

Let $R(t)$ be the multistate rate matrix at time $t$.
Off diagonal element $R_{ij}(t;x) = \hat\lambda{ij}(t;x)$ contains the estimate 
transition rate between state $i$ and state $j$, at time $t$, for a subject
with covariate vector $x$.  
To normalize $R$ set its diagonal element such that row sums are 0. 
(This is just what has to be done to compute the matrix exponential, no serious
theory is involved wrt the normalization step.)
If we assume a set of time points 
$t_0, t_1, t_2, ...$ such that the transition rates are constant over each
interval $t_i \le t < t_{i+1}$ the the probability in estimate satisfies
\begin{equation}
 p(t_i; x) = p(t_0) e^{(t_1-t_0)R(t_0;x)} e^{(t_2-t_1)R(t_1;x)} \ldots 
 e^{(t_i-t_{i-1})R(t_{i-1};x)} \label{simplehmm}
\end{equation}
where $p(t)$ is a vector with one element per state, $p_k(t)$ the probability of
being in state $k$, and $p(t_0)$ is the probability vector for the starting
state, normally all zeros with a single ``1'' corresponding to the starting 
state.
The exponential of a matrix, e.g. $\exp(R)$, is dealt with at length in the
mathematics vignette. Suffice it to say that compuation of this quantity, and
of its derivative, is more complex than the exponential of a single value
\cite{Moler78, Moler03, Mohey09}.

In the semiparametric MSH first the parameter estimates $\hat\beta$ are 
computed based on a partial likelihood, predictions $p(t;z)$ for any chosen
covariate vector $z$ are computed at the very end.
In the interval censored MSH $p(t;x)$ is computed during maximization, that is,
for each trial solution of $\hat\beta$ and every $x$.
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Compute the predicted curve $p(t;x_i)$ for each subject $i$
  \item Compare the prediction to the observed state for subject $i$, at each
    observation time $t_{ij}$ for that subject.
  \item Use this to compute an updated $\hat\beta$.
\end{enumerate}

The computation of a likelihood in cmsh is a sum over subjects, 
and each subject can be computed independently.  (We take advantage of this
and use parallel computation if possible, either set mc.cores using 
\code{options}  or include it in the cmsh call.)
The algorithm for the likelihood of each subject alternates two steps.  
First step forward to a new
time point using the matrix exponential as in equation \eqref{simplehmm}.
Whenever a subject is observed, restrict their $p(t)$ by setting to 0 all of the
``states where they ain't'', i.e., all elements of $p$ except the 
observed state.
The mathematics vignette has more formal details.

\section{Partially observed states}
\begin{figure}
<<mcsa1, echo=FALSE>>=
mstate <- c("CU", "MCI", "dementia", "death")
mmat <- matrix(0, 4, 4, dimnames=list(mstate, mstate))
mmat[1,2] <- mmat[2,3] <- 1
mmat[1:3,4] <- 1
statefig(rbind(3,1), mmat)
@  
 \caption{State space model for the dementia data.}
 \label{mcsastate}
\end{figure}

The simplest extension of the simple interval censored model, and the simplest
hidden Markov model, involves a partially observed state.
An example appears in the Mayo Clinic Study of Aging, a blinded subset of which
is present in the \code{dementia} data set.
Per protocol, enrolled subjects have return visits every 15 months, at each
visit they are diagnosed as being the cognitively unimpaired (CU), mild
cognitive impairment (MCI), or dementia state, leading to the multi-state
model shown in figure \ref{mcsastate}.

Subjects who have not returned in 36 months or more are flagged for a record
review and their medical history is retrieved. (Due to the resources of the
Rochester Epidemiolgy Project, we have access to the medical records of all
participants in the county, across all providers of care.) 
From that review a status, at their most recent physician visit, 
of demented vs non-demented (ND) can be ascertained, 
but CU versus MCI cannot be distinguished.
Study visits include additional cognitive testing, which will not have been
part of a standard office visit.

The modification of the likelihood is simple.  At such a visit, if the
diagnosis is ``dementia'', then all elements of $p(t)$ for that subject, except
state 3 (dementia) are set to 0.  For a non-demented diagnosis, elements 3
and 4 (dementia and death) are set to 0, the other two are unchanged.
We treat the off study diagnosis as a \emph{marker}, our
label for an imperfect assessment of the underlying state.
The data will have three distinct outcome variables: death, observed state
at a study visit, and partial state at an ordinary office visit. 

<<dement1>>=
states <- c("CU", "MCI", "dementia", "death")
dqmat <- matrix(0, 4, 4, dimnames=list(from= states, to=states))
dqmat[1,2] <- dqmat[2,3] <- 1
dqmat[1:3, 4] <- 1
dqmat
# statefig(rbind(3,1), dqmat)  # generate the state figure

table2 <- function(...) table(..., useNA= "ifany")  # show NA in tables
with(dementia, table2(studydx, offdx))

# Create a missclassification table for the off study diagnosis
vmiss <- cbind(c(1,1,0), c(0,0,1))
dimnames(vmiss) <- list(state= states[1:3], visit=c("ND", "dementia"))

# create a state variable for death + on study visits
temp <- with(dementia, ifelse(is.na(studydx), 4*death, as.numeric(studydx)))
# At this time, don't allow backward transitions, we'll get to that later
temp <- unlist(tapply(temp, dementia$ptnum, cummax))
dstate <- factor(temp, 0:4, c("censor", "CU", "MCI", "dementia", "death"))

if (FALSE){
dfit1 <- cmsh(Surv(age, dstate) ~ apoepos + male, qmatrix= dqmat,
             data= dementia, id = ptnum,
             marker= list(state:offdx ~0 /discrete(init=vmiss)))
}
@ 

The above is not yet finished.

\subsection{CAV example}
Data from a study that monitors the progression of coronary allograft
vasculopathy (CAV) is provided in the msm package. 
CAV is a post-transplant deterioration of the aterial walls in patients who
have recieved a heart transplant. 
Make a few changes to
the data set to set it up for the cmsh function, and show some summaries. 
First fit CAV outcome as interval censored. 

<<newcav>>=
library(msm)
cav2 <- cav
names(cav2) <- casefold(names(cav2))  # lower case (personal preference)

# The model
cstates <- c("no CAV", "mild/mod", "severe", "death")
cqmat <- matrix(0, 4, 4, dimnames=list(cstates, cstates))
cqmat[1,2] <- cqmat[2,1] <- cqmat[2,3] <- cqmat[3,2] <- 1
cqmat[1:3,4] <- 1
statefig(rbind(3,1), cqmat, offset=.02)

#  multi-state status for Surv(), first level has to be "no event at this time"
cav2$cstate <- factor(cav2$state, 0:4,  
                      c("alive", "no CAV", "mild/mod", "severe", "death"))
table(cav2$cstate)       # no off study FU!
with(cav2, table(from= cstate[duplicated(ptnum, fromLast=TRUE)],
                 to = cstate[duplicated(ptnum)]))[-1,]
table(table(cav2$ptnum)) # number with 1, 2, 3, ... visits
@

We use a standard multi-state Surv(time, state) form of the survival
package, where the state variable is a factor such that
the first level of the factor represents ``no event at this time''.
The CAV data is unusual in that there are no censored observations, 
each interval ends in one of the 4 states.
The 622 subjects have betwen 2 15 assessments, 27\% with 2 and 21\% with 3.
The transitions table above shows the count of observed transtition pairs, a 
subject with 5 visits will contribute 4 counts to the table.
Remaining in the no CAV state accounts for the largest number of visit
pairs. As should be the case, there are no transitions from death to another
state.

As seen below, the inter-visit intervals vary widely, a situation that favors
an interval censored model.  

<<cinterval>>=
# what are the inter-visit intervals?
i1 <- duplicated(cav2$ptnum, fromLast=TRUE)
i2 <- duplicated(cav2$ptnum)
interval <- cav2$years[i2]- cav2$years[i1]
ideath <- cav2$cstate[i2]=="death" # did the interval end in death?
#between visits
hist(interval[!ideath], nclass=50, main=NULL,
     xlab="Time between consecutive visits (years)")
#hist(interval[ideath], nclass=30, main=NULL,
#     xlab="Years between last visit and death")
@

For interval censored data we need to specifiy the set of
valid transitions $Q$.  This is a matrix of 0/1 values, which can
also be used by the statefig routine. 
The row/column names of the transition matrix are the names of the states,
levels in the Surv object need to match these.
<<cavstates>>=
cqmat

cfit0 <- cmsh(Surv(years, cstate) ~ 1, data= cav2, id= ptnum,
               qmatrix= cqmat, exact="death")
cfit0  #msm vignette, 2.5, model 1
@

For the above model states are of two types: the
state is known at the measurment time but not when that state was entered,
and exact states, where the entry time to the state is known. These latter
are enumerated in the \code{exact} argument.
Death is the most common exact state.   
One can also have multiple exact states, one of the more common examples 
is cause of death, e.g., states of `death due to cancer' and `other death'
had been separated by the user.
The status variable in the Surv call is a factor with censored as the
first level, followed by the states, in the same order as the $Q$ matrix.
Now add covariates.

<<cav2>>= 
cfit1 <- cmsh(list(Surv(years, cstate) ~ 1, 
                  1:2 + 1:4 ~ sex),
             data=cav2, id= ptnum, init= cfit0,
             qmatrix= cqmat)
cfit1  # msm vignette, 2.7, model 2a
@

The code allows the use of a prior model for the initial values.
Interestingly, the number of iterations needed to estimate the initial
intercept was larger than for \code{cfit1}. This is not uncommon, in
our experience.

<<cav2b>>=
cfit2 <- cmsh(list(Surv(years, cstate) ~ dage, 
                  1:2 + 2:3 ~ sex / common,
                  2:1 + 3:2 ~ sex / common,
                  0:death   ~ sex / common),  data=cav2, id= ptnum,
             qmatrix= cqmat, exact="death", init=cfit0)
cfit2  

cfit2$cmap
@ 

More complex models are specified using a list for the formula argument.
The first formula in the list specifies the response and a default set of
covariates which is used as the starting point for all transtitions.
In the above example age at diagnosis \code{dage}.
Later formulas update the formula(s) for specified transtions
\begin{itemize}
  \item line 2 of cfit1 adds sex to the formulas for none to mild (1:2) and
    none to death (1:4), but not to other transitions
  \item line 2 of cfit2 forces the the 1:2 progression
    (no CAV to mild/moderate) and 2:3 (mild/moderate to severe) to share a
    common sex coefficient
  \item line 3 of cfit2 makes the sex coefficients for the two reverse 
    transitions identical
  \item 0:death stands for any state to death. Transitions marked as 0 in
    qmatrix are ignored whenever exanding a shorthand like this, i.e., 
    a death:death transtion
\end{itemize}

The \code{cmap} component of the returned fit is the overall map between
covariates, linear predictors, and coefficients.
The final fit has 17 unique coefficients, the linear predictor for the 3:4
(severe:death) transition for example makes use of coefficients 16, 17 and 13.



\section{What is a hidden Markov model (HMM)?}
The answer to this is that HMM is a general technique, and we see the
label applied to many different problems in many different subfields.
The cmsh package addresses a particular structure, one that has been useful
in our analyses of data from the Mayo Clinic Study of Aging (MCSA).

Our models can be thought of having two levels.  Level 1 is a multi-state
hazards model, much like those addressed by multistate hazard (MSH) models
fit using the coxph function in the survival library.
Here is a moderately complex model for illustration, based on a dementia study. 
<<states>>=
states <- c(outer(paste0("A", 0:1), paste0("C", 0:2), paste, sep='.'), "death")
statedata <- data.frame(state= states,
                        A=c(0,1, 0,1, 0,1, 2),
                        N=c(0,0, 1,1, 2,2, NA),
                        D=c(0,0, 0,0 ,0,0, 1))
statedata
qmat <- qplus(statedata, death= "death")
statefig(cbind(2,2,2,1), qmat, alty=rep(1:2, c(7,8)))
@ 

The above model assumes two latent processes, amyloid accumulation in the brain,
A0 and A1,
and cognitive decline from no impairment to high, C0, C1, C2.
The premise of an HMM is that these states are not directly observed, rather
what we see are biomarkers that indicate, imperfectly, the underlying process.
The latent or ``hidden'' states operate in continuous time, so transitions in
the figure are a change in A or N, never both at once.  
Because of intermittent observation a subject might well be in A0:N0 at one
visit and A1:N2 at the next, but the unobserved continuous process would not
have made an instantaneous multistate jump.

Here is a possible specification for a model.  
It is purposely a very general one.

<<echo=TRUE, eval=FALSE>>=
mfit <- cmsh(list(Surv(time, death) ~ male,
                 0:death ~ iage,
                 A(0):A(1) ~ apoe + male + ns(iage,4)/ common,
                 C(0):C1(1) + C(1):C(2) ~ iage + cmc),
            marker = list(C:dx ~ 1/ multinomial(pattern= pmat),
                          C:memory~ 1 /logistic,
                          C:memory~ practice/ logistic(param=mean) + common,
                          A(1:2):centiloid + A(1:2):sqrt(p.tau181) ~1/gaussian),
            data= dementia, id=ptnum, qmatrix = qmat, 
            statedata = statedata)
@ 

Full estimation includes 13 transition rates (arrows), along with the 
relationship
of 4 biomarkers (dx, memory, centloid, and p.tau181) to the underlying
states. It is a complex model, with a lot of parameters, and the 
specification is also complex. The statedata data set makes specification a bit 
easier by setting up aliases, e.g., A(0) is shorthand for A0:C0, A0:C1 and
A0:C2. 
The data set has a row per state, in the same order as they are found in
the transition matrix \code{qmat}.  It's first column must be a variable
named ``state'', the other columns provide aliases.

The specification for the 13 transitions rates has the same form as in the
coxph function.  Each of the rates is
 modeled as $\exp(\beta X)$, in the
same manor as a Cox model, each transition has it's own equation.
The formula argument, the first argument of the cmsh function, is a list
whose first element is the default formula for each transiton, subsequent lines
each update the formula for selected transitions.
Here is a sample from the above:
\begin{itemize}
  \item All 6 transitons to death are of the form \code{~ male + iage}; each 
    has separate coefficients
  \item The A0C0:A1C0, A0C1:A1C1 and A0C2:A1C2 transitions, the three vertical
    arrows in the figure, have the form \code{~ male + apoe + ns(iage,4)};
    these three transitions have the same coefficients.
  \item The four C transitions (horizontal arrow) have form \code{~ iage + cmc}
\end{itemize}
Behind the scenes the A(0):A(1) specification translates as any valid transition
from a state where the A column of statedata is 0, to one where the A
column is 1.  
Going from row 1 (A0.C0) to row 6 (A1.C2) formally satisfies the A(0) to A(1)
pattern, but is not one of the valid transitions, per qmat.

The marker specification is also a list of formulas. The 
\code{C:memory ~ /gaussian}
specification states that each of the true cognitive states C has an expected
distribtuion of memory scores which follows a logistic distribution: true
state C(0) gives rise to logistic($\mu_1$, $\sigma_1$) memory scores,
true state C(1) gives rise to logistic($\mu_2$, $\sigma_2$) memory scores,
and true state C(3) to logistic($\mu_3$, $\sigma_3$). This gives rise to
6 linear predictors in the model, one for each mean and log(std) value.
Memory scores tend to be systematically better when the same test is 
readministered a second time, this practice effect is often coded as a factor
with levels of 0, 1-2, 3+ for the number of prior tests.
The second memory line states that the linear predictors for $\mu_1-\mu_3$
will also include the practice effect, and that this effect is assumed to
be the same size for all 3 latent states.
(An assumption that is open to challenge; a C2 subject might not remember prior
test exposures as well.) 

A latent state of none/low amyloid, A(0), is assumed to lead to 
gaussian distributions for centloid values and for log(tau), 
PET based biomarkers, and ditto for A(1). The A(3) row, death, will never
correspond to a PET measurement.
This leads to 2 markers * 2 state groups * 2 parameters = 8 linear predictors.
In this case each LP is simply an interecept.
The diagnosis variable is categorical, leading to a multinomial distribution
for the state:marker combination. The pattern argument will be illustrated in
a later example.




The first major difference between the MSH and HMM models is that observations
on each state come from interval censored data, also known as panel data. 
That is, subjects visit at regular intervals and undergo a panel of tests.
We will know their state at a given test date, but not the time at which the
study participant transitioned to that state.  This leads to a more complex
likelihood than a MSH model, but also more realistic for data such as this.

The second difference is that an HMM treats the states as latent factors.
The model will have standard covariates, things
like age, sex and genotype which affect the transition rates from state to
state, and also reporter or biomarker variables on the states themselves.
In this case, we might have the following misclassification matrix.
\begin{centering}
  \begin{tabular}{ccccc}
   & \multicolumn{4}{c}{Observed} \\
True & CU & MCI & dementia & death \\
C0   & $1- p_1$& $p_1$ & 0 & 0 \\
C1   & $p_2$ & $1-(p_2 + p_3)$ & $p_3$ & 0 \\
C2   & 0     & $p_4$ & $1-p_4$ & 0 \\
death& 0 & 0 & 0 & 1
 \end{tabular} \end{centering}

We have been careful above to use one set of labels for the true states
(C0, etc) and another for the observed states.  
In many cases a user will be tempted to use the same labels for the true
underlying state and for the clinical observation: this is a good way to
generate confusion, and we recommend that you do otherwise.
Death is a special state, it is not interval censored as we
know the exact death date, nor is it a latent state since it is observed
without error.

A primary reference for the material is Christopher Jackson's excellent manual 
for the msm package \cite{Jackson16}.
Others that I found particularly helpful were a web page by Nikolai
Shokiriv \cite{Shokhirev}, Satten and Lingini \cite{Satten96} and
computational details from Kalbfleisch and Lawless \cite{Kalbfleisch85}.

Per Shokhirev there are 3 canonical problems for HMM models.

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Given the model parameters, compute the probability of a particular
    output sequence.  This is solved by the Forward or Backwards algorithms.
  \item Given the model parameters and an output sequence, compute the
    most likely path for the hidden states (the true but unobserved path).
    This is solved by the Viterbi algorithm.
  \item Given an output sequence, find the most likely set of model
    parameters.  This is solved by the Baum-Welch algorithm.
\end{enumerate}
We are focused on problem 1: an underlying search algorithm will choose
parameters $\beta$, which map to rates, which in turn lead to a likelihood
value.  The search algorithm uses this to find an MLE.
Many references, e.g. Wikipedia, focus only on problem 2 the exclusion of
the others.  This led to much early confusion for this author, e.g., 
the Viterbi algorithm involves a forward recursion and earns a 'forward'
label as well.
It might look like 3 is our target, but that would only be true if there 
were no covariates and we were directly estimating the rates.

Further math and computational details are found in a mathematical vignette.

\section{Design}
Specification of an HMM model is complex, because the model is complex.
We need to specify
\begin{itemize}
  \item The underlying Markov model: the states, state names, and the
    allowable transitions.
  \item Which covariates apply to each transition, and which of these
    transition/covariate pairs share a coefficient.
  \item For each marker $y$  transmission function $P(y |s)$ needs to be
    defined, which provides the distribution of $y$ given the true state $s$.
    (Death does not need a transmission function).
  \item The initial probabilities, whether these should be estimated,
    and if so what covariates to use.
  \item Initial values for the coefficients and specification of which of them,
    if any, are fixed.
\end{itemize}


One of the attractions of the HMM likelihood is that reporter
variables need not all be present.  In the MCSA for instance, visits are
scheduled every 15 months, but PET imaging is only gathered on a subset,
and for many of those PET is done every 30 months. For the HMM likelihood there
is no need to ``fill in'' those other measurement using imputation or 
other special
missing value computations, etc.  Each measured reporter adds to the likelihood
and, those which are not measured at this visit don't.  Simple.


\section{Time dependent covariates}
Though markers for the states can be observed intermittently, covariates
that affect the rates can not be missing, the same restriction that applies
in a MSH model.  
If cerebral vascular disease (CeVD) were the state, for instance, with an 
intermittently observed predictor of the disease rate such as systolic blood
pressure, the SBP would commonly be filled in wrt continuous time by using 
the most
recently measured value, also called last value carried forward; see the 
\code{lcvf} function. 


There are 3 kinds of states in the hmm fitter:
latent states, which are not observed precisely but for which we have observed
but imperfect \emph{markers} of the current state, interval censored states,
where the
state a subject is in at the observation time is certain, but not when they
entered the state, and exact states where both the state and its time of entry
is known. Both interval censored and exact states should appear as levels 
in the in the second argument to Surv, \code{cstate} above,
which must be a factor whose first level corresponds to ``no observed state
at this time'' and remaining levels will match the state labels in the
$Q$ matrix. (Common labels for this first level are ``censor'' or ``none'').
Death is the most common exact state, so much so that ``death'' is the default 
value of the \code{exact} argument, and so we could have omitted it
from the above call.

The above fits have no latent states, so the observed outcome is captured in
the Surv argument.  
The formula argument specifies the models for the between state transitions,
and has the same form as in the coxph function of the survival package.
It is a list where the first formula is the default, the left hand side is the
response and the right the covariates.  Further elements of the list have
the form of one or more state:state transitions on the left and covariates on
the right.  Behind the scense there is a formula for each transition, each
starts as the default and is modified by each later addition or subtraction,
using the same logic as \code{update.formula}.  
The \code{common} option states that the specified transitions share a
common \code{sex} coefficient.
The result of the formulas is captured in \code{cmap} component of the fit,
each positive integer is an index into the overall coefficient vector.
The following are valid specifications of transitions:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \code{0:death}, \code{0:state('death')}, \code{0:4}: transition from
    any other state to death.  Illegal transition are silently removed, i.e.,
    this does not include death:death.
  \item \code{c(1:3):4}, \code{state('no CAV', 'mild/mod', 'severe'):death}:
    same as the above
  \item \code{''no CAV'':''mild/mod'' + ''mild:mod'':''severe''}:
    same as \code{1:2 + 2:3}
\end{itemize}
    
The default for cmsh is to not maximize, returning the result at
the initial parameters.  The second two calls use what we have found to be
the most robust option for general practice, which is based on a 
Levenburg-Marquart algorith along with a trust region, and uses the outer
product of the score vectors $U_i$ per subject as an approximate Hessian
matrix. 



\section{Example data}
We will use the included \code{dementia} data set as a running example for
the remainder of the vignette.  This data set is a simulant, based on data
from the Mayo Clinic Study of Aging which was used in the analyses of 
\cite{Jack2022} and \cite{Jack2025}.
The MCSA is an age and sex stratified sample of subject from Olmsted County,
Minnesota.  After recruitment, subjects are invited to return for a study
visit every 15 months, with the goal of understanding the evolution of
cognitive decline along with various markers of the pathology that underlies
such decline.  
The \code{dementia} data set focuses on two processes. 
The first is cognitive loss, as
measured by clinical assessment, cognitively unimpaired (CU) versus
mild cognitive impairment (MCI) versus dementia, a memory score based on a
battery of cognitive tests, and hippocampal volume, a measure the of neural 
loss that presumably underlies cognitive decline.
The second is progression of amyloid and tau deposition, known
to be part of the Alzheimers disease pathway, direct PET imaging of amyloid and
tau in the brain, and a putative blood based biomarker P-tau181 of amyloid load.
(Whether amyloid and tau are causative the disease or only convient markers
process is vigorously debated.)
Death is a competing risk for both processes.

\section{Multi-state hazard model}
We first fit a multi-state hazard (MSH) model, using functions from the
survival package. This will help make the data clearer, along with the
differences between MSH, interval censored and HMM models.
The MSH fit will partially reprise the analysis in \cite{Jack2022}, 
which fits the simple
3 state model of not demented (ND)/dementia/death on age scale, with sex,
 amyloid, CMC, and APOE positivity as covariates.

The choice of time scale is a critical step in the analysis plan. One useful
way to think about this is as a choice of which covariates to explicitly
model and which covariates to adjust for by matching. 
The advantage of matching on some covariate $x$ is that it provides the best 
possible adjustmement: it works independently of the true functional form of the
relationship of the relationship $y = f(x)$.  A disadvantage is that we do not
get a p-value, or an estimate of $f$.  
In epidemiology studies of chronic disease, 
matching on age and sex is often a natural
choice: we already know that rates rise with age and differ by sex, no one
needs a p-value ``proof'' of this, and a given study is usually focused on other
questions. 
In a simple Cox model or a multistate hazard (MSH) model, the partial likelihood
is based on matching. If time-since-enrollment is used as the underlying scale
then for an observed dementia in subject ``Smith'', say, at 249 days post 
enrollment, Smith is compared to all others who were also alive and at 
risk at 249 days post enrollment, using their appropriate covariate values 
at that time.
If age is used as the time scale and that same event happened when Smith was
30104 days of age (approx 82y 8m), the matched group will be all those who were
active in the study at 30104 days of age, some of whom might have been enrolled
in the study for a month while others had been active for a decade.
A general rule of thumb for the Cox model is to match on the time variable
that is the most impactfull and/or difficult to model, and model the easier
and/or less impactful time scale. 
I often tell the researcher ``Assume you want to predict a subject's risk over
the next year, and you can have only one of age, time 
since first diagnosis of desease, or time since enrollment in the study to
guide you.  Which would you choose?''


Both death and dementia rates rise rapidly with
age, death rates are approximately log-linear from age 50 onward 
but dementia rates have a different form (a later but more precipitous rise).
The MCSA is is an age and sex stratified random sample from a defined population,
enrollment ages range from 50 to 90 and follow-up of up to 16 years.  
The data thus has observations from 50 to 100+ years of age, over this range 
population death rates increase by approximately 150 fold, e.g.,
\code{survexp.mn[100,,'2020']/ survexp.mn[50,,'2020']}.
If modeling the effect of age on the hazard one really 
needs to get it right, blindly adding \code{+ age} the the formula will not
suffice, since even a small error can cause effects of interest, such as the
1.3--1.4 fold effect APOE genotype, to be completely lost in the resulting bias.
On the other hand, one would expect little clinical effect for time since
enrollment, which is the time since a subject was randomly selected from the
master age/sex registry of Olmsted County residents for an invitation letter.
This makes age the natural time scale for an MSH model.

A model using time from enrollment as the time scale would include age as
a covariate; similarly a model using age as the time scale should include
time-since enrollment as a covariate. 
Based on prior experience we have coded this as a categorical time-dependent
variable of 0-1 years, 1-4, and 4+ years post enrollment, with 4+ as the
reference level.
The dementia data set included in the package is modeled on the actual MCSA
data, results will not match any analyses using the actual MCSA sample but
will often be similar in magnitude.  


